content.text:
Not yet in the room, no. The item exists in the local uncommitted source as `last_stand_circlet`, but:

- it only enters newly generated runs;
- it is in the rare equipment loot pool, so it is not guaranteed;
- an existing run will not be retroactively repaired;
- the current runtime may need a reload after the source changes.

Its effect is: below 50% HP, add 25% crit chance before the crit-bag draw.

And yes, your resource idea is strong. ECS-js resources are runtime-only by default and are explicitly suited to world-local, non-persisted state. A general `pseudoRandomBag` resource could own:

- per-actor/channel bag state;
- shuffle-bag, weighted-bag, pity, or other strategies;
- reset behavior at world/session boundaries;
- deterministic seeded draws.

The component approach is better for state that belongs in the world model and needs querying, saving, or inspection. For ephemeral crit-bag state, a resource may be the cleaner abstraction. I’d let this playtest validate the behavior first, then consider migrating the implementation into a reusable resource.

